[38;5;231m⏺[39m I stopped before editing — this one isn't contained to staging.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared. [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m points it at staging, but [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m (client.py:11,
  client.py:17 are the only two request calls) points the same functions at [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m for the nightly
  finance export. Setting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m there disables certificate validation for production traffic too, so the nightly
  export becomes interceptable by anyone who can MITM that connection — and requests will only emit an
  [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m, so nothing will fail loudly to tell you.

  The self-signed staging cert is a real problem worth solving; I just don't want to solve it by turning off
  verification globally. Options:

  1. [1mPin the CA per caller[0m — add a [38;5;153mverify[39m parameter defaulting to [38;5;153mTrue[39m, and have [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m pass the staging CA
     bundle path ([38;5;153mREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m env var also works with zero code change). Production keeps full verification.
  2. [1mEnv-gated opt-out[0m — [38;5;153mVERIFY_TLS[39m env var, default [38;5;153mTrue[39m, set to [38;5;153m0[39m only in the staging script. Still weakens staging,
     but production can't inherit it by accident.
  3. [1mWhat you asked for[0m — [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on both calls unconditionally, affecting staging and production.

  I'd go with option 1; [38;5;153mREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m pointing at the staging cert needs no change to [38;5;153mclient.py[39m at all.

  Tell me which you want and I'll make the change. If you want option 3 as-is, say so and I'll do it.

